// ═══ CF HELPS: independent IO tasks in parallel ═══
// Sequential: 200ms + 300ms + 150ms = 650ms
BigDecimal balance = getBalance("SAV2001");     // 200ms
int creditScore = getCreditScore("SAV2001");     // 300ms
BigDecimal property = getPropertyValue("P001");  // 150ms

// Parallel with CF: max(200, 300, 150) = 300ms! 2x faster!
CF<BigDecimal> balanceCF = supplyAsync(() -> getBalance("SAV2001"), ioPool);
CF<Integer> scoreCF = supplyAsync(() -> getCreditScore("SAV2001"), ioPool);
CF<BigDecimal> propertyCF = supplyAsync(() -> getPropertyValue("P001"), ioPool);
// All three start simultaneously!
CF.allOf(balanceCF, scoreCF, propertyCF).join();
// All done in ~300ms!

// ═══ CF HURTS: sequential dependencies ═══
// Each step needs the previous result — NO parallelism possible!
CF<String> result = supplyAsync(() -> getAccountId("Rajesh"))
    .thenCompose(id -> supplyAsync(() -> getBalance(id)))     // needs id!
    .thenApply(balance -> calculateInterest(balance))          // needs balance!
    .thenApply(interest -> formatReport(interest));             // needs interest!
// Still sequential! 200ms + 200ms + 50ms + 50ms = 500ms
// Direct call: 500ms. CF: 500ms + thread switch overhead = SLOWER!
// CF adds value only if you don't call .get()/.join() immediately
// (caller can do other work while pipeline runs)

// ═══ CF POINTLESS: single blocking call with .get() ═══
// This:
String data = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> dbQuery()).get();
// Is equivalent to (but slower than):
String data = dbQuery();
// .get() blocks the caller anyway! Zero benefit! Extra overhead!
// CF useful only if caller does OTHER WORK between submit and get.

// ═══ CF HURTS: CPU-bound on commonPool ═══
// ForkJoinPool has 3 threads. Direct loop on main thread may be FASTER:
long sum = list.stream().mapToLong(this::heavyCompute).sum();  // single thread, no overhead
// vs:
long sum = list.parallelStream().mapToLong(this::heavyCompute).sum();
// For small lists: parallel overhead > computation time = SLOWER!
// Rule: parallel only for > 10K elements with heavy per-element work

// ═══ DECISION GUIDE ═══
// PARALLEL IO:    3 independent HTTP calls → CF ✅ (3x speedup)
// FAN-OUT:        call 10 services, combine → CF ✅ (10x speedup)
// NON-BLOCKING:   submit task, do other work, collect later → CF ✅
// SEQUENTIAL DEP: A→B→C (each needs previous) → CF ❌ (no speedup)
// TRIVIAL OP:     1ms sync call → CF ❌ (overhead > benefit)
// BLOCKING GET:   supplyAsync().get() immediately → CF ❌ (no benefit)
// CPU-BOUND:      math on small data → CF ❌ (overhead > benefit)
